WEBVTT

1
00:02:29.337 --> 00:02:31.077
Give me that horse.

2
00:02:31.717 --> 00:02:33.537
He couldn't go on.

3
00:02:33.737 --> 00:02:35.217
Nor could you.

4
00:02:36.537 --> 00:02:37.997
You're injured, man.

5
00:02:38.077 --> 00:02:39.457
Let us take you in the house.

6
00:02:39.537 --> 00:02:40.877
I gotta go on.

7
00:02:40.977 --> 00:02:42.859
Gotta get the telegraph station.

8
00:02:44.237 --> 00:02:45.657
We'll take you.

9
00:02:46.097 --> 00:02:47.097
Penelope!

10
00:03:14.217 --> 00:03:16.057
Yeah.

11
00:03:44.277 --> 00:03:45.697
Sorry, close down for the night.

12
00:03:45.777 --> 00:03:46.977
I know, but this is urgent.

13
00:03:47.057 --> 00:03:48.837
Won't you please? No,
come out of there, you.

14
00:03:48.917 --> 00:03:50.117
Can't go against my schedule.

15
00:03:50.197 --> 00:03:52.217
Close down. But can't you
make this one exception?

16
00:03:52.297 --> 00:03:53.977
Nope. Get in here. What?

17
00:03:54.057 --> 00:03:56.817
Bring a light with you.
You can't do this to me.

18
00:03:56.897 --> 00:03:58.477
I won't stand for it.

19
00:03:58.557 --> 00:04:00.577
The Union Pacific won't stand for it.

20
00:04:00.657 --> 00:04:02.857
Night prowling around here like this.

21
00:04:02.937 --> 00:04:05.537
You've got your ball busting in my office.

22
00:04:05.737 --> 00:04:07.457
Who do you think you are anyway?

23
00:04:07.537 --> 00:04:09.177
My key's closed down for the night.

24
00:04:09.257 --> 00:04:11.277
Well, you can write it,
but I won't send it anyway.

25
00:04:11.357 --> 00:04:12.537
Here, let me.

26
00:04:14.977 --> 00:04:17.357
Territorial Recorder's Office.

27
00:04:18.137 --> 00:04:19.977
Fort Lansing.

28
00:04:21.797 --> 00:04:27.637
The final corner stakes on Blotts 234,

29
00:04:27.717 --> 00:04:30.017
235.

30
00:04:30.537 --> 00:04:32.637
Chart 1089.

31
00:04:33.397 --> 00:04:35.477
Record in my name.

32
00:04:35.657 --> 00:04:38.457
That's all. How shall I sign it?

33
00:04:38.617 --> 00:04:39.837
Court Evans.

34
00:04:42.137 --> 00:04:44.037
Oh!

35
00:05:22.837 --> 00:05:24.637
All set, Mr. Evans.

36
00:05:29.677 --> 00:05:32.137
Why, he ain't dead, is he?

37
00:05:33.877 --> 00:05:35.697
No, just knocked out.

38
00:05:37.237 --> 00:05:39.617
So that's Quirt Evans.

39
00:05:40.157 --> 00:05:41.557
Look at his gun.

40
00:05:41.837 --> 00:05:43.661
See how many notches he's done.

41
00:05:44.317 --> 00:05:46.137
Not any of them I can see.

42
00:05:46.217 --> 00:05:48.517
I sure ain't gonna touch it and find out.

43
00:05:48.597 --> 00:05:51.126
He might wake up and
take it the wrong way.

44
00:05:51.457 --> 00:05:54.497
Well, when he and Wyatt Earp
were deputies over the tombstone,

45
00:05:54.577 --> 00:05:57.812
the two of them shot it out
of the whole Clanton bunch.

46
00:05:58.697 --> 00:06:00.997
So that's Quirt Evans.

47
00:06:01.357 --> 00:06:02.297
Hmm.

48
00:06:02.377 --> 00:06:04.318
He was quite a man with the gals.

49
00:06:05.197 --> 00:06:08.432
the eyes of any a man and
open the eyes of any a woman.

50
00:06:09.557 --> 00:06:11.597
Would you call my father please?

51
00:06:11.677 --> 00:06:12.737
Yes ma'am.

52
00:06:20.377 --> 00:06:22.577
Where are we taking him? To the wagon.

53
00:06:22.657 --> 00:06:23.557
Take him home.

54
00:06:23.637 --> 00:06:25.777
Quirt Evans? You're crazy.

55
00:06:26.177 --> 00:06:29.001
I'd soon have a black
powder bomber in my house.

56
00:06:30.037 --> 00:06:31.437
Heavy, ain't he?

57
00:06:31.837 --> 00:06:34.277
I know a dozen men riding night and day.

58
00:06:34.357 --> 00:06:36.717
Captain Quirt Evans helped us like this.

59
00:06:36.797 --> 00:06:37.897
Get him in there.

60
00:06:40.377 --> 00:06:41.997
I've heard about you people.

61
00:06:42.077 --> 00:06:45.397
How you don't believe in guns or
anything like that, only doing good.

62
00:06:45.477 --> 00:06:48.657
So take my advice. You'll
stay plenty far away from Mr.

63
00:06:48.737 --> 00:06:49.497
Quirt Evans.

64
00:06:49.577 --> 00:06:51.557
Thank you very much for your advice.

65
00:06:51.637 --> 00:06:52.517
Good night.

66
00:06:52.597 --> 00:06:53.597
Good night.

67
00:06:56.577 --> 00:06:58.237
So long, Quirt.

68
00:06:59.437 --> 00:07:02.157
That Quirt Evans is sure of something.

69
00:07:02.637 --> 00:07:03.997
Quirt Evans?

70
00:07:04.537 --> 00:07:06.037
You know him?

71
00:07:06.117 --> 00:07:07.797
Do I know Quirt Evans?

72
00:07:07.877 --> 00:07:10.097
Do I know Quirt Evans?

73
00:07:10.757 --> 00:07:12.357
What a question.

74
00:07:15.077 --> 00:07:16.137
No.

75
00:07:16.857 --> 00:07:17.857
Sam.

76
00:07:19.117 --> 00:07:22.557
Don't. If that doesn't quiet
him, I don't know who it will.

77
00:07:22.637 --> 00:07:25.537
I'll never get that bullet out
of him kicking around like that.

78
00:07:25.617 --> 00:07:28.029
I'm afraid to give him any more sedative.

79
00:07:28.457 --> 00:07:29.777
enough. Please be quiet.

80
00:07:29.857 --> 00:07:32.057
The doctor's trying to...
He can't hear you.

81
00:07:32.137 --> 00:07:35.197
What do you suppose that
delirious mind is searching for?

82
00:07:35.277 --> 00:07:38.637
He must be in great pain. Not
with that much laudanum in him.

83
00:07:38.717 --> 00:07:41.057
It's as if he were reaching for something.

84
00:07:41.137 --> 00:07:42.617
Something specific.

85
00:07:42.937 --> 00:07:44.697
I don't understand it.

86
00:07:44.777 --> 00:07:47.057
By now, he should be completely inert.

87
00:07:47.137 --> 00:07:48.517
I didn't think...

88
00:07:48.597 --> 00:07:49.737
No.

89
00:07:50.317 --> 00:07:51.437
All right.

90
00:07:51.757 --> 00:07:52.897
All right.

91
00:07:52.997 --> 00:07:54.217
All right.

92
00:08:04.017 --> 00:08:07.817
Thomas how's the young man delirious

93
00:08:27.517 --> 00:08:32.217
a gun in this house Thomas but
they put these back in the gun belt

94
00:08:38.637 --> 00:08:41.637
Maybe we'll have to get
something and tie him down.

95
00:08:42.077 --> 00:08:43.457
Are you crazy?

96
00:08:43.537 --> 00:08:44.637
It's empty.

97
00:08:49.197 --> 00:08:51.277
So that was it. A gun.

98
00:08:51.357 --> 00:08:52.357
It's a pity.

99
00:08:52.437 --> 00:08:56.837
It's stupid. These wild ones, I keep
picking bullets out of them and setting their

100
00:08:56.917 --> 00:08:58.037
bones. Why?

101
00:08:58.117 --> 00:09:00.717
It's their destiny to
wind up on Boot Hill.

102
00:09:00.797 --> 00:09:01.797
No.

103
00:09:02.617 --> 00:09:04.457
Oh, I forgot.

104
00:09:04.777 --> 00:09:06.597
Never speak evil, huh?

105
00:09:07.237 --> 00:09:11.957
Well, get me some more hot water, young
lady, and we'll start patching him up for

106
00:09:12.037 --> 00:09:13.797
whatever's in store for him.

107
00:09:16.517 --> 00:09:18.399
Come on, help me with this boat.

108
00:09:37.897 --> 00:09:38.837
Mother.

109
00:09:38.917 --> 00:09:39.917
Hmm?

110
00:09:40.897 --> 00:09:42.357
Tell me again.

111
00:09:43.477 --> 00:09:44.777
What, dear?

112
00:09:45.297 --> 00:09:47.137
Oh, about meeting Father.

113
00:09:47.217 --> 00:09:50.517
Oh, they've heard that a thousand times.

114
00:09:51.237 --> 00:09:54.497
He was working on the roof
of the new meeting house.

115
00:09:54.597 --> 00:09:58.277
He fell off, and I picked him out
of the dust and held him in my arms.

116
00:09:58.357 --> 00:10:02.017
He always said I hugged him
before I ever even knew his name.

117
00:10:02.097 --> 00:10:03.717
And they both knew right away?

118
00:10:03.797 --> 00:10:05.097
Of course.

119
00:10:10.817 --> 00:10:14.641
Of course, I knew he was a peaceful
man, a member of the society.

120
00:10:14.977 --> 00:10:18.637
An evil man would seek a more profitable
way of life than being a member of the

121
00:10:18.717 --> 00:10:19.717
friends.

122
00:10:19.937 --> 00:10:22.037
Father had a beard then, didn't he?

123
00:10:22.137 --> 00:10:23.137
Yes.

124
00:10:23.837 --> 00:10:25.837
I should think that would scratch.

125
00:10:26.937 --> 00:10:29.277
I prefer a man clean-shaped.

126
00:10:32.477 --> 00:10:35.397
He's got the constitution
of a mountain lion.

127
00:10:35.477 --> 00:10:39.137
He'll pull through,
barring blood poisoning.

128
00:10:39.277 --> 00:10:43.797
These wild ones never seem to get blood
poisoning, though it's common enough among

129
00:10:43.877 --> 00:10:46.417
the godly. You're so-called atheists.

130
00:10:46.497 --> 00:10:49.637
You always feel so compelled
to stretch your godlessness.

131
00:10:49.717 --> 00:10:51.897
Wash him. The bullet's out.

132
00:10:55.737 --> 00:10:57.617
He's in no danger.

133
00:10:58.517 --> 00:11:00.497
I bet he killed a lot of people.

134
00:11:00.577 --> 00:11:02.317
No, he didn't. How does Zeno?

135
00:11:02.397 --> 00:11:03.457
That's enough, John.

136
00:11:03.537 --> 00:11:05.772
A friend doesn't speak of such things.

137
00:11:09.497 --> 00:11:11.597
Mrs. Worth, get that man out of here.

138
00:11:11.677 --> 00:11:13.457
But he's badly injured, Doctor.

139
00:11:13.537 --> 00:11:14.917
This isn't civilized Pennsylvania.

140
00:11:14.997 --> 00:11:16.717
This is a raw frontier.

141
00:11:16.797 --> 00:11:18.817
You must take a realistic attitude.

142
00:11:18.897 --> 00:11:22.737
This is a place where mayhem, theft, and
murder are the commonplace instead of the

143
00:11:22.817 --> 00:11:25.937
unusual. Would that justify
leaving a wounded man to die?

144
00:11:26.017 --> 00:11:29.782
Build your house by the side of
the road and be a friend to man.

145
00:11:30.317 --> 00:11:32.057
Ye who believe.

146
00:11:32.177 --> 00:11:34.097
I don't mind your mocking.

147
00:11:34.177 --> 00:11:35.797
You've been a good friend to us.

148
00:11:35.877 --> 00:11:37.637
I'm glad I have a logical mind.

149
00:11:37.717 --> 00:11:39.257
And a good heart.

150
00:11:39.337 --> 00:11:40.317
Good night, Doctor.

151
00:11:40.397 --> 00:11:41.397
Good night.

152
00:11:47.697 --> 00:11:49.637
Still having delirious spells.

153
00:11:49.717 --> 00:11:51.617
Keeps mumbling about his past.

154
00:11:51.697 --> 00:11:53.157
I can get him.

155
00:11:53.517 --> 00:11:56.497
I'm afraid he's had a very violent life.

156
00:11:56.817 --> 00:11:58.637
Very good.

157
00:11:59.937 --> 00:12:02.597
God, sir. Stand back.

158
00:12:03.317 --> 00:12:05.777
Copper of my bet on the black ace.

159
00:12:07.457 --> 00:12:08.737
Yeah, good.

160
00:12:10.037 --> 00:12:12.457
You're a shapely husband.

161
00:12:13.937 --> 00:12:17.357
You get real confirmation, Margaret.

162
00:12:17.837 --> 00:12:19.117
Huh?

163
00:12:20.497 --> 00:12:23.537
You fill out the address just right.

164
00:12:25.177 --> 00:12:26.937
That's your color.

165
00:12:28.017 --> 00:12:29.777
All right, Margaret.

166
00:12:32.637 --> 00:12:34.657
Chart 1089.

167
00:12:35.177 --> 00:12:36.437
1089.

168
00:12:36.617 --> 00:12:38.617
Margaret, you're a nuisance.

169
00:12:39.057 --> 00:12:41.637
Let's call it off, Margaret.

170
00:12:42.077 --> 00:12:45.557
Yeah, I got places to go
and country to put behind.

171
00:12:46.137 --> 00:12:47.817
Sure, sure.

172
00:12:47.897 --> 00:12:48.977
All right.

173
00:12:56.617 --> 00:12:57.717
Lila.

174
00:12:58.457 --> 00:12:59.557
Lila.

175
00:13:04.237 --> 00:13:09.417
If I own the mint, I put
your head on every coin.

176
00:13:11.857 --> 00:13:15.617
I used to smoke corn
silk when I was a kid.

177
00:13:16.837 --> 00:13:19.457
Your hair is just like that, Lyla.

178
00:13:20.777 --> 00:13:22.297
Light brown,

179
00:13:22.797 --> 00:13:24.917
yellow all mixed up.

180
00:13:25.657 --> 00:13:28.217
Just like corn silk, Lyla.

181
00:13:31.317 --> 00:13:33.057
All gold,

182
00:13:33.697 --> 00:13:34.997
torn.

183
00:13:40.897 --> 00:13:42.797
Will you bring some hot coffee, Penny?

184
00:13:42.877 --> 00:13:45.757
You know, the doctor says
he's completely out of danger.

185
00:13:45.837 --> 00:13:47.297
Just sleeping.

186
00:13:47.377 --> 00:13:50.717
You know, Dr. Penny considers
him her own personal patient.

187
00:13:50.797 --> 00:13:52.697
Sit down. And will you
bring some milk, dear?

188
00:13:52.777 --> 00:13:55.717
And when you're finished, you can go up
and watch him while you cowered your wool on

189
00:13:55.797 --> 00:13:57.737
the loom. He doesn't need watching.

190
00:13:57.817 --> 00:14:00.057
He's had no temperature
these past few days.

191
00:14:00.137 --> 00:14:04.897
Just a superlatively healthy animal,
restoring his depleted strength with sleep.

192
00:14:04.977 --> 00:14:06.797
He's no longer delirious.

193
00:14:06.877 --> 00:14:09.377
But of course, if you're
determined to watch over him, Penny,

194
00:14:09.457 --> 00:14:12.633
perhaps you'd better take
a pencil and paper with you.

195
00:14:12.877 --> 00:14:16.457
words should be recorded, they may
be of great interest to history.

196
00:14:16.537 --> 00:14:19.137
Or more possibly to a
United States Marshal.

197
00:14:19.217 --> 00:14:23.937
Who knows what violence is involved with
his battered frame and his bullet holes.

198
00:14:24.017 --> 00:14:25.717
Would you care for a donut, Doctor?

199
00:14:25.797 --> 00:14:27.097
They're not as light as they should be.

200
00:14:27.177 --> 00:14:32.137
Listen, Mrs. Worth, you can carry this
head in the sand attitude just so far in a

201
00:14:32.217 --> 00:14:33.557
world of reality.

202
00:14:33.777 --> 00:14:36.037
Let me get this man out of your house.

203
00:14:36.117 --> 00:14:39.637
I can dump him in the establishment
that passes for a hotel in this oft-times

204
00:14:39.717 --> 00:14:41.497
dubious community of ours.

205
00:14:41.577 --> 00:14:43.317
In that unsanitary hovel?

206
00:14:43.397 --> 00:14:44.977
Then he'll be off your hands.

207
00:14:45.057 --> 00:14:46.897
But he's much better off here.

208
00:14:46.977 --> 00:14:49.657
Of course he is, but who
cares what happens to him?

209
00:14:49.737 --> 00:14:50.737
We do.

210
00:14:52.217 --> 00:14:53.977
I don't know what to say.

211
00:14:58.277 --> 00:15:01.477
Once, when I was studying
medicine in Europe, I had a friend,

212
00:15:01.557 --> 00:15:02.357
an artist.

213
00:15:02.437 --> 00:15:06.257
He drew portraits of people and made them
resemble the animals they reminded him of.

214
00:15:06.337 --> 00:15:10.037
he'd have drawn this
man as a coiled cobra.

215
00:15:10.337 --> 00:15:15.957
Oh, doctor, your analogy is terribly
imperfect and your naturalism faulty.

216
00:15:16.217 --> 00:15:17.777
Cobras don't coil.

217
00:15:18.497 --> 00:15:24.257
Now, but doctor, we're so fond of
you and we respect you so greatly.

218
00:15:24.437 --> 00:15:29.817
We're sure that you will finally realize
that realism, untempered by sentiment and

219
00:15:29.897 --> 00:15:34.217
humanity, is really just a mean,
hard, cold outlook on life,

220
00:15:34.297 --> 00:15:35.957
a frightened outlook.

221
00:15:40.297 --> 00:15:41.997
I stand defeated.

222
00:15:42.757 --> 00:15:47.537
And furthermore, there are times in this
house when I feel as if I were living in a

223
00:15:47.617 --> 00:15:49.157
never-never land.

224
00:15:49.857 --> 00:15:53.337
But don't hesitate to call
on me anytime you need help.

225
00:16:22.997 --> 00:16:24.537
Hello.

226
00:16:25.017 --> 00:16:26.357
You're awake.

227
00:16:26.437 --> 00:16:27.457
Sure.

228
00:16:27.917 --> 00:16:29.817
I'm awake.

229
00:16:32.797 --> 00:16:35.037
What is all this?

230
00:16:35.117 --> 00:16:36.197
What?

231
00:16:36.337 --> 00:16:37.677
Where am I?

232
00:16:39.037 --> 00:16:40.457
Oh, don't you remember?

233
00:16:40.537 --> 00:16:42.577
Father and I drove you into town.

234
00:16:49.317 --> 00:16:50.937
Is anything wrong?

235
00:16:51.157 --> 00:16:52.157
No.

236
00:16:56.337 --> 00:16:59.455
The doctor says you'll be
all right in a week or two.

237
00:17:03.257 --> 00:17:05.237
You were delirious, you know.

238
00:17:06.337 --> 00:17:07.937
Two days and two nights.

239
00:17:10.197 --> 00:17:12.373
Well, maybe that's why I'm so hungry.

240
00:17:14.797 --> 00:17:16.777
I could eat a yearling steer.

241
00:17:18.397 --> 00:17:20.117
If I could catch one.

242
00:17:20.257 --> 00:17:22.257
Oh, well, we'll get you something.

243
00:17:23.337 --> 00:17:24.337
Mother!

244
00:17:24.937 --> 00:17:27.077
You talked a lot in your delirium.

245
00:17:28.977 --> 00:17:30.757
Mother, he's awake. How is he?

246
00:17:30.837 --> 00:17:32.117
Hungry. Oh, he's hungry.

247
00:17:32.197 --> 00:17:33.785
He's going to be all right.

248
00:17:36.597 --> 00:17:38.297
Thanks a lot for housing me.

249
00:17:38.377 --> 00:17:39.457
You're welcome.

250
00:17:39.877 --> 00:17:42.537
Smoke sausage and eggs? Sounds great.

251
00:17:42.677 --> 00:17:44.777
Two eggs, or would you like three?

252
00:17:44.857 --> 00:17:48.077
I'd like six. Oh, you shall have them.

253
00:17:49.297 --> 00:17:53.017
Penny, get one of father's
shirts and a razor.

254
00:17:53.117 --> 00:17:54.777
I'll get him some food.

255
00:17:59.497 --> 00:18:01.397
So I talked a lot.

256
00:18:01.477 --> 00:18:02.377
Yes.

257
00:18:02.457 --> 00:18:05.557
I listened only because I thought you
might say something that would aid us in

258
00:18:05.637 --> 00:18:08.757
helping you. As to notifying
your wife, for instance?

259
00:18:08.837 --> 00:18:11.017
What did I say? If you had a wife.

260
00:18:11.097 --> 00:18:12.937
What did I talk about?

261
00:18:13.097 --> 00:18:15.597
Well, you talked of Margaret.

262
00:18:15.777 --> 00:18:19.217
She, uh, she filled out a red dress.

263
00:18:19.437 --> 00:18:20.797
What else?

264
00:18:21.337 --> 00:18:24.197
Oh, you mentioned someone named, uh,

265
00:18:24.977 --> 00:18:25.977
Lila?

266
00:18:26.597 --> 00:18:27.737
That all?

267
00:18:27.817 --> 00:18:30.417
If you have a wife, we could notify her.

268
00:18:30.497 --> 00:18:31.817
No wife.

269
00:18:38.477 --> 00:18:39.777
That's twelve.

270
00:18:40.757 --> 00:18:44.197
I haven't been so flattered since my
donuts won first prize at the fair back in

271
00:18:44.277 --> 00:18:45.037
Pennsylvania.

272
00:18:45.117 --> 00:18:47.176
They must be smart in Pennsylvania.

273
00:18:47.257 --> 00:18:50.517
Some of the members thought it an
exhibition of pride to enter my donuts.

274
00:18:50.597 --> 00:18:53.157
But what harm can there
be in a little donut?

275
00:18:53.237 --> 00:18:56.897
Unless one eats so many of them that they
explode, which is likely to happen to you,

276
00:18:56.977 --> 00:18:59.177
young man. Well, there's
worse ways of checking out.

277
00:18:59.257 --> 00:19:00.337
I'll help you with the dish.

278
00:19:00.417 --> 00:19:01.077
No.

279
00:19:01.157 --> 00:19:03.117
Please stay and watch Kurt Evans.

280
00:19:03.197 --> 00:19:06.837
I'm curious to know the effect
of six eggs, a pound of sausage,

281
00:19:06.917 --> 00:19:08.117
and 12 donuts.

282
00:19:08.197 --> 00:19:10.717
I'll call in time for the milking.

283
00:19:13.897 --> 00:19:16.877
Do all you people from
Pennsylvania talk like that?

284
00:19:16.957 --> 00:19:18.157
Like what?

285
00:19:18.957 --> 00:19:20.017
The?

286
00:19:20.497 --> 00:19:22.957
Oh, well, we're friends.

287
00:19:23.197 --> 00:19:25.037
Friends of who? Of all.

288
00:19:25.117 --> 00:19:26.437
The Society of Friends.

289
00:19:26.517 --> 00:19:27.997
Many people call us Quakers.

290
00:19:28.077 --> 00:19:29.457
Oh, it's a religion.

291
00:19:29.537 --> 00:19:30.777
It's a belief.

292
00:19:32.617 --> 00:19:37.637
That on the wall, that each human being
has an integrity that can be hurt only by

293
00:19:37.717 --> 00:19:42.397
the act of that same human being, and
not by the act of another human being.

294
00:19:42.477 --> 00:19:44.737
Is that Quaker stuff? Mm-hmm.

295
00:19:45.017 --> 00:19:46.957
You mean nobody can hurt you but yourself?

296
00:19:47.037 --> 00:19:48.697
That's a friend's belief.

297
00:19:49.177 --> 00:19:52.397
Well, supposing somebody whacks you
over the head with a branding iron,

298
00:19:52.477 --> 00:19:53.617
won't that hurt?

299
00:19:53.997 --> 00:19:55.257
Physically, of course.

300
00:19:55.337 --> 00:19:59.657
But in reality, it would injure only the
person doing the act of force or violence.

301
00:19:59.737 --> 00:20:02.737
Only the doer can be hurt
by a mean or evil act.

302
00:20:03.357 --> 00:20:05.337
Are there very many of you Quakers?

303
00:20:05.417 --> 00:20:06.657
Very few.

304
00:20:07.477 --> 00:20:09.017
Sort of figured that.

305
00:20:40.917 --> 00:20:41.917
Morning, gentlemen.

306
00:20:41.997 --> 00:20:42.997
Morning.

307
00:20:43.197 --> 00:20:44.557
Carrot from a garden.

308
00:20:44.637 --> 00:20:46.657
I found these out in the road.

309
00:20:46.917 --> 00:20:49.388
They belong to a fellow named Quirt Evans.

310
00:20:49.537 --> 00:20:51.117
I understand you're a friend of his.

311
00:20:51.197 --> 00:20:52.737
I thought maybe you'd see
that he gets them back.

312
00:20:52.817 --> 00:20:54.617
Leave him here. I'll see if he gets them.

313
00:20:54.697 --> 00:20:55.997
That Quirt.

314
00:20:56.097 --> 00:20:58.744
He'd lose his shirt if
it wasn't buttoned on.

315
00:20:58.917 --> 00:21:00.446
Careless about everything.

316
00:21:00.877 --> 00:21:02.197
I keep telling him.

317
00:21:02.277 --> 00:21:03.917
Sooner or later, he...

318
00:21:07.877 --> 00:21:11.177
On second thought, you can just tell me
where he is, and I'll take him on back to

319
00:21:11.257 --> 00:21:12.257
him myself.

320
00:21:13.217 --> 00:21:14.717
Where is Quirt Evans?

321
00:21:15.817 --> 00:21:17.997
You mean, where is he?

322
00:21:19.177 --> 00:21:22.217
You ever notice how a fellow keep repeating
the question when he's stalling about

323
00:21:22.297 --> 00:21:23.097
answering?

324
00:21:23.177 --> 00:21:23.877
Yeah.

325
00:21:23.957 --> 00:21:26.837
Maybe if I bend this gun over
his head, he'll quit stalling.

326
00:21:26.917 --> 00:21:29.077
Well, gentlemen,
I hardly know Quirt Evans.

327
00:21:29.157 --> 00:21:29.837
Uh-oh.

328
00:21:29.917 --> 00:21:31.337
He lies, too.

329
00:21:31.677 --> 00:21:35.501
You've been bragging all over
town what good friends you two are.

330
00:21:35.797 --> 00:21:38.197
You know, this fella ain't got
many good qualities, has he?

331
00:21:38.277 --> 00:21:39.617
Sit down, friend.

332
00:21:40.057 --> 00:21:41.297
Maybe that'll help your memory.

333
00:21:41.377 --> 00:21:42.937
I'm asking about Quirt Evans.

334
00:21:43.017 --> 00:21:45.217
Well, all I know is...

335
00:22:02.357 --> 00:22:04.197
Thanks for sewing the shirt.

336
00:22:04.277 --> 00:22:05.917
But I can't find my pants.

337
00:22:05.997 --> 00:22:09.617
Get back in bed. Oh, laziness is the
only thing that's kept me there this long.

338
00:22:09.697 --> 00:22:12.737
The doctor says you're to
stay in bed four more days.

339
00:22:12.817 --> 00:22:14.637
Oh, Audie, we're... Oh, you're up.

340
00:22:14.717 --> 00:22:16.717
That's no way to speak
to an older person, Joe.

341
00:22:16.797 --> 00:22:17.897
Well, we're pals.

342
00:22:17.977 --> 00:22:20.977
You wouldn't expect a pal
to call me mister, would you?

343
00:22:21.057 --> 00:22:22.397
See, Dr.

344
00:22:22.477 --> 00:22:25.197
Mangrove says to tell you some
men in town are looking for you.

345
00:22:25.277 --> 00:22:26.577
Says one of the men was a Mr.

346
00:22:26.657 --> 00:22:27.917
Laredo-Steven.

347
00:22:30.197 --> 00:22:32.237
Get my pants. But your legs.

348
00:22:32.317 --> 00:22:34.157
Johnny, get me my pants. No, John.

349
00:22:34.237 --> 00:22:35.857
Will he get my pants?

350
00:22:40.157 --> 00:22:41.857
Get the pants, John.

351
00:22:42.777 --> 00:22:44.437
What you said.

352
00:22:49.037 --> 00:22:50.517
What are you looking at?

353
00:22:51.617 --> 00:22:52.617
The.

354
00:22:55.417 --> 00:22:58.337
Oh, boy, it's a good
thing I'm not a tattletail.

355
00:23:00.577 --> 00:23:02.257
I said him, woman.

356
00:23:03.317 --> 00:23:05.457
They used the familiar in speaking to me.

357
00:23:05.537 --> 00:23:07.157
The what? The familiar.

358
00:23:07.357 --> 00:23:09.077
The plain language.

359
00:23:09.437 --> 00:23:11.197
Get me my pants, they said.

360
00:23:11.277 --> 00:23:12.297
Well, what about it?

361
00:23:12.377 --> 00:23:15.237
Well, among us, thee and thou
are used only to love once.

362
00:23:15.317 --> 00:23:18.477
To all others, we use you and he and they.

363
00:23:18.557 --> 00:23:21.792
Mother to children, husband
to wife, and children to...

364
00:23:23.577 --> 00:23:25.577
Between lovers, of course.

365
00:23:27.577 --> 00:23:28.777
I'm sorry.

366
00:23:28.857 --> 00:23:30.117
Oh, I'm not.

367
00:23:30.877 --> 00:23:31.677
What?

368
00:23:31.757 --> 00:23:34.377
I'm not. I wish you'd speak so to me.

369
00:23:52.377 --> 00:23:53.397
Well.

370
00:23:53.497 --> 00:23:54.957
I knew right away.

371
00:23:55.037 --> 00:23:56.157
That night.

372
00:23:58.797 --> 00:24:01.517
This must be a kind of a dull life.

373
00:24:01.697 --> 00:24:03.537
I can take you to a lot of places.

374
00:24:03.617 --> 00:24:04.617
I'd go.

375
00:24:04.797 --> 00:24:05.677
What?

376
00:24:05.757 --> 00:24:09.017
I'd rather stay here with Eve,
but if he'd rather go someplace else,

377
00:24:09.097 --> 00:24:10.744
then I'll go someplace else.

378
00:24:15.197 --> 00:24:17.197
That sounds very bold.

379
00:24:18.257 --> 00:24:20.357
Well, it's just that, uh,

380
00:24:20.937 --> 00:24:25.577
I didn't think religious people
were quite so, uh, sudden and direct.

381
00:24:25.657 --> 00:24:29.775
Well, it's the way I feel, and it'd
be dishonest to say anything else.

382
00:24:31.557 --> 00:24:32.577
Yeah.

383
00:24:33.497 --> 00:24:35.337
I used to wonder about it.

384
00:24:35.417 --> 00:24:37.497
I suppose every girl does.

385
00:24:38.017 --> 00:24:39.157
About what?

386
00:24:39.537 --> 00:24:40.977
Oh, about love.

387
00:24:41.057 --> 00:24:44.557
About whether as you got to know someone,
you fell in love little by little,

388
00:24:44.637 --> 00:24:46.997
or it happened all at once.

389
00:24:47.777 --> 00:24:49.217
They are shocked.

390
00:24:49.817 --> 00:24:51.157
Well, love.

391
00:24:51.877 --> 00:24:55.757
I wouldn't say, uh,
shocked exactly, but, uh,

392
00:24:57.497 --> 00:24:59.017
you sure get to the point.

393
00:24:59.097 --> 00:25:00.637
Should I be different?

394
00:25:00.817 --> 00:25:03.697
No, Penny, I like you
just the way you are.

395
00:25:04.357 --> 00:25:06.217
Who emptied this gun?

396
00:25:06.297 --> 00:25:07.297
Father.

397
00:25:08.277 --> 00:25:11.697
Court, a gun is a
symbol of force, of evil.

398
00:25:12.217 --> 00:25:14.397
We never have one in our homes.

399
00:25:14.477 --> 00:25:17.397
It was a concession that Father
allowed even an empty one.

400
00:25:17.477 --> 00:25:19.317
It was only because you
couldn't rest without it.

401
00:25:19.397 --> 00:25:20.577
Why the cartridges?

402
00:25:21.357 --> 00:25:22.357
Outside?

403
00:25:38.637 --> 00:25:40.517
Hey, why don't we just bust in?

404
00:25:40.597 --> 00:25:44.477
Because busting through doors with Quirt
Evans on the other side ain't my idea of a

405
00:25:44.557 --> 00:25:46.057
healthy pastime.

406
00:25:47.697 --> 00:25:49.257
Tell him to come in.

407
00:25:56.537 --> 00:25:57.637
Come in.

408
00:26:01.357 --> 00:26:03.397
You can stand right there.

409
00:26:04.737 --> 00:26:06.157
Against the light.

410
00:26:08.677 --> 00:26:09.877
Howdy, Quirt.

411
00:26:12.917 --> 00:26:15.329
You sure run a ring around me, all right.

412
00:26:15.717 --> 00:26:18.877
But you know, I don't think it was right
friendly of you to beat me to locating that

413
00:26:18.957 --> 00:26:21.077
tract after my boys found it.

414
00:26:21.577 --> 00:26:23.697
Of course, I'm willing to
listen to your side of it.

415
00:26:23.777 --> 00:26:25.317
I'm a reasonable man.

416
00:26:25.397 --> 00:26:26.397
Sure.

417
00:26:26.537 --> 00:26:28.037
Laredo's always reasonable.

418
00:26:28.117 --> 00:26:30.657
That's why he's the biggest
man in the territory.

419
00:26:30.737 --> 00:26:33.561
You're the biggest man
in the territory, Laredo?

420
00:26:34.017 --> 00:26:35.637
So a lot of people say.

421
00:26:35.717 --> 00:26:38.197
I hadn't heard. You nervous?

422
00:26:39.257 --> 00:26:40.617
No, Evans.

423
00:26:40.977 --> 00:26:45.057
I can't figure out why you keep
stepping on my toes all the time.

424
00:26:45.137 --> 00:26:47.019
Of course, that's your business.

425
00:26:47.157 --> 00:26:48.677
I'm a good loser.

426
00:26:49.137 --> 00:26:50.577
I like a good loser.

427
00:26:50.657 --> 00:26:53.277
Of course, I like any kind of
a loser better than a winner.

428
00:26:53.357 --> 00:26:54.357
Yeah.

429
00:26:54.537 --> 00:26:57.577
You know, I had a chance to do a heap of
thinking while I was following your trail

430
00:26:57.657 --> 00:26:58.937
across the territory.

431
00:26:59.237 --> 00:27:02.943
I followed it by that string of
run-out horses you left behind.

432
00:27:03.377 --> 00:27:05.789
And well, sir, I figured it out this way.

433
00:27:06.077 --> 00:27:08.157
Quirt don't really want that land.

434
00:27:08.457 --> 00:27:10.717
Because work ain't exactly in his line.

435
00:27:10.797 --> 00:27:13.217
Is it in your line? No, of course not.

436
00:27:13.297 --> 00:27:15.356
I was getting it for another fella.

437
00:27:15.697 --> 00:27:18.377
And, well, I figure maybe you'll sell.

438
00:27:18.837 --> 00:27:20.937
I don't hear anything jingle.

439
00:27:21.837 --> 00:27:24.437
Is it all right if I put
my hand inside my shirt?

440
00:27:24.517 --> 00:27:25.637
Why not?

441
00:27:32.597 --> 00:27:34.537
Five thousand in gold there.

442
00:27:34.817 --> 00:27:36.257
I want twenty.

443
00:27:37.837 --> 00:27:40.257
Well, okay, but I'll
have to owe you fifteen.

444
00:27:40.337 --> 00:27:41.690
That's all I got on me.

445
00:27:41.837 --> 00:27:43.877
I got the deed in my pocket here.

446
00:27:44.397 --> 00:27:45.717
Get it out.

447
00:27:47.337 --> 00:27:51.161
Now, if the young lady could find
us something to sign this with,

448
00:27:57.417 --> 00:28:00.477
Still Laredo's hired boy, eh, Hondo?

449
00:28:00.837 --> 00:28:02.657
Picking up after him?

450
00:28:02.737 --> 00:28:05.177
Carrying matches for his cigars?

451
00:28:05.257 --> 00:28:07.917
You'll mind yourself. He'll mind himself.

452
00:28:07.997 --> 00:28:09.397
Won't you, Hondo?

453
00:28:09.477 --> 00:28:10.477
Miss,

454
00:28:10.817 --> 00:28:12.317
would you mind taking us over to Mr.

455
00:28:12.397 --> 00:28:13.457
Evans, please?

456
00:28:28.737 --> 00:28:29.757
Thank you.

457
00:28:31.057 --> 00:28:34.217
You know, I'm sure glad to
see you up and around, Quirt.

458
00:28:34.297 --> 00:28:36.356
We heard you was flat on your back.

459
00:28:36.717 --> 00:28:38.557
Sorry to disappoint you.

460
00:28:38.777 --> 00:28:42.306
And you come on over and get
the rest of that money anytime.

461
00:28:42.537 --> 00:28:44.713
Anytime, that is, that you feel able.

462
00:28:45.777 --> 00:28:47.817
I'll see you one of these days.

463
00:28:48.457 --> 00:28:50.637
Well, uh, so long, Quirt.

464
00:29:14.197 --> 00:29:16.317
Settle up my horse, will you, kid?

465
00:29:16.677 --> 00:29:18.377
And throw on my war bags.

466
00:29:18.457 --> 00:29:20.837
What would have happened if
they knew the gun was empty?

467
00:29:20.917 --> 00:29:23.741
It would have been Saturday
night in Sioux City.

468
00:29:24.917 --> 00:29:26.917
Tie on my slicker, Tilt.

469
00:29:33.397 --> 00:29:34.397
Sorry.

470
00:29:35.277 --> 00:29:37.037
Oh, no, Kurt, no.

471
00:29:37.217 --> 00:29:38.937
What kind of a border do you think I am?

472
00:29:39.017 --> 00:29:40.477
You can't do that.

473
00:29:40.677 --> 00:29:43.297
We'd tend any wounded
human, and not for pay.

474
00:29:43.377 --> 00:29:47.577
You don't believe in force,
so you can't force me to take it back.

475
00:29:47.717 --> 00:29:49.797
I'm afraid you're outwitted, Thomas.

476
00:29:49.877 --> 00:29:51.517
How long have I been here?

477
00:29:51.597 --> 00:29:53.137
Almost three weeks.

478
00:29:53.397 --> 00:29:55.017
Kind of hate to get going.

479
00:29:55.097 --> 00:29:57.517
Oh, but you can't. Your leg,
you're in no condition to travel.

480
00:29:57.597 --> 00:30:01.437
Don't worry. Your cooking's got
me grained up and ready for market.

481
00:30:01.517 --> 00:30:02.697
Adios, amigos.

482
00:30:08.737 --> 00:30:09.737
Quirt.

483
00:30:13.697 --> 00:30:15.557
Shall I pack my things?

484
00:30:16.217 --> 00:30:17.237
Why?

485
00:30:18.217 --> 00:30:19.997
They'd leave without me.

486
00:30:20.697 --> 00:30:22.397
I got places to go.

487
00:30:22.477 --> 00:30:26.536
Court, I wouldn't care. It doesn't
make any difference to me how far.

488
00:30:29.557 --> 00:30:30.577
Oh.

489
00:30:33.617 --> 00:30:34.817
I know so little.

490
00:30:34.897 --> 00:30:35.917
I...

491
00:30:36.817 --> 00:30:40.170
I didn't know what could
happen to one and not the other.

492
00:30:41.717 --> 00:30:44.217
I thought it always happened to both.

493
00:30:52.177 --> 00:30:53.677
That isn't it.

494
00:30:55.277 --> 00:30:56.497
It isn't?

495
00:30:56.577 --> 00:30:58.917
Well, uh... Then they do feel that...

496
00:30:58.997 --> 00:31:02.197
Look, Penny, uh...

497
00:31:02.657 --> 00:31:05.716
I'm not the kind of a guy
that does things suddenly.

498
00:31:06.657 --> 00:31:09.897
I, uh, gotta look before I leave.

499
00:31:09.977 --> 00:31:11.617
You can ask anybody.

500
00:31:11.697 --> 00:31:16.057
They'll tell you that Quirt Evans
is a mighty cautious citizen.

501
00:31:16.877 --> 00:31:20.617
Oh, then it's just that
they're not sure yet.

502
00:31:22.237 --> 00:31:23.457
Yeah.

503
00:31:24.177 --> 00:31:25.397
Sergeant.

504
00:31:26.657 --> 00:31:28.237
Please stay.

505
00:31:29.297 --> 00:31:31.357
He's all settled, Quirt.

506
00:31:33.897 --> 00:31:35.557
I'm saddling.

507
00:31:39.377 --> 00:31:40.897
You gonna stay?

508
00:31:44.597 --> 00:31:46.177
A double eagle.

509
00:31:47.097 --> 00:31:49.457
Quid, there's something
I'd rather have than this.

510
00:31:49.537 --> 00:31:51.517
A favor, a big favor.

511
00:31:51.597 --> 00:31:53.437
Well, you got them both. What is it?

512
00:31:53.517 --> 00:31:55.397
Would you ride past
the schoolhouse with me?

513
00:31:55.477 --> 00:31:58.517
There's a lot of boys who
don't believe I know Quid Evans.

514
00:31:58.597 --> 00:31:59.597
Sure.

515
00:31:59.937 --> 00:32:03.837
Oh, golly, everybody says you're
the fastest man in the territory.

516
00:32:07.017 --> 00:32:11.057
Well, there's those
who'd say I'm pretty slow.

517
00:32:17.697 --> 00:32:21.017
A man should be able to do a good
day's work after breakfast like that.

518
00:32:21.097 --> 00:32:22.777
I'll see that he do it.

519
00:32:23.437 --> 00:32:24.437
Penny,

520
00:32:25.037 --> 00:32:27.117
where is the bucket? Oh,

521
00:32:27.357 --> 00:32:30.533
I declare, Penny, I don't
know what's happening to me.

522
00:32:30.637 --> 00:32:34.737
Funny thing about pancakes, I lose my
appetite for them after the first couple of

523
00:32:34.817 --> 00:32:37.993
dozen. They weren't very
good this morning, too heavy.

524
00:32:40.397 --> 00:32:41.817
Wild Indian.

525
00:32:42.417 --> 00:32:45.277
Put water on those
vegetables this morning.

526
00:32:45.357 --> 00:32:47.837
There's always work
around here to be done.

527
00:32:47.917 --> 00:32:49.817
They wanted to be a farmer, didn't they?

528
00:32:49.897 --> 00:32:51.757
Not anymore, I don't.

529
00:33:00.917 --> 00:33:03.277
Surely you can walk to
the barn without that.

530
00:33:03.357 --> 00:33:03.937
What?

531
00:33:04.017 --> 00:33:05.217
The gun.

532
00:33:05.397 --> 00:33:07.837
Oh, well, it balances me.

533
00:33:07.917 --> 00:33:09.157
One leg's longer than the other.

534
00:33:09.237 --> 00:33:10.637
You know, the weight.

535
00:33:11.157 --> 00:33:12.957
They are a liar.

536
00:33:24.857 --> 00:33:26.697
Throw the cow some hate.

537
00:33:29.117 --> 00:33:30.717
There's a pitchfork.

538
00:33:33.837 --> 00:33:36.897
Before I was 15, I learned
to hate those things.

539
00:33:36.977 --> 00:33:40.437
I swore I'd leave plows
and pitchforks to farmers.

540
00:33:41.637 --> 00:33:42.997
I'll do it.

541
00:33:43.077 --> 00:33:44.637
No, I'll do it.

542
00:33:44.857 --> 00:33:46.617
But it's an occasion.

543
00:33:49.817 --> 00:33:50.877
These.

544
00:33:51.257 --> 00:33:52.637
What?

545
00:33:53.317 --> 00:33:56.057
Nothing, just...

546
00:33:56.257 --> 00:33:58.077
just thee.

547
00:34:02.917 --> 00:34:04.817
Penny!

548
00:34:23.557 --> 00:34:25.937
Well, you wanted hay, didn't you?

549
00:34:26.857 --> 00:34:28.117
Didn't thee?

550
00:34:30.997 --> 00:34:32.017
Fifteen?

551
00:34:32.617 --> 00:34:35.557
Better put this littlest one in the box.

552
00:34:41.437 --> 00:34:43.357
Now get a bucket of water.

553
00:34:43.437 --> 00:34:46.677
Water, water. All I do is carry water.

554
00:34:46.777 --> 00:34:48.657
Uh-uh. No force.

555
00:34:51.457 --> 00:34:53.557
Say, wasn't this place irrigated?

556
00:34:53.637 --> 00:34:56.337
I see all these flumes and ditches.

557
00:34:56.737 --> 00:34:58.657
Frederick Carson turned the water off.

558
00:34:58.737 --> 00:35:00.117
Frederick Carson?

559
00:35:00.677 --> 00:35:04.197
He's the man who bought the ranch up above
from friends who couldn't make a go of

560
00:35:04.277 --> 00:35:04.777
it.

561
00:35:04.837 --> 00:35:08.997
We had built a community dam,
but it was on his property.

562
00:35:09.077 --> 00:35:10.417
Well, what'd you do about it?

563
00:35:10.497 --> 00:35:11.657
Don't tell me.

564
00:35:11.737 --> 00:35:12.997
You prayed.

565
00:35:13.077 --> 00:35:14.057
Of course.

566
00:35:14.137 --> 00:35:15.417
Get you any water?

567
00:35:15.497 --> 00:35:16.497
Well,

568
00:35:16.677 --> 00:35:18.457
we didn't pray for water.

569
00:35:18.537 --> 00:35:20.977
We prayed for Frederick Carson.

570
00:35:22.457 --> 00:35:25.577
Carson turned off the water
and you prayed for him?

571
00:35:25.737 --> 00:35:27.117
Of course.

572
00:35:27.357 --> 00:35:28.797
Can't you see?

573
00:35:28.877 --> 00:35:32.230
By committing an evil act,
the poor man injured his soul.

574
00:35:33.977 --> 00:35:35.737
Glad there's a well.

575
00:35:35.977 --> 00:35:39.797
Because it'd sure get thirsty around
here if we had to wait for Carson.

576
00:35:39.877 --> 00:35:41.577
Gonna walk this pony.

577
00:35:54.477 --> 00:35:55.437
Benny?

578
00:35:55.517 --> 00:35:56.517
Hmm?

579
00:35:58.197 --> 00:35:59.197
Nothing.

580
00:36:10.097 --> 00:36:12.417
Be sure you don't leave
no slack in that cinch.

581
00:36:12.497 --> 00:36:14.077
The boss will sure howl.

582
00:36:14.157 --> 00:36:16.677
Yeah, this ain't the
first pony I ever saddled.

583
00:36:16.757 --> 00:36:18.777
He'd squawk no matter how I done it.

584
00:36:18.857 --> 00:36:21.328
He sure getting mean these days, ain't he?

585
00:36:21.937 --> 00:36:24.217
Cantankerous by his natural bornin.

586
00:36:24.297 --> 00:36:27.121
That boy on his neck
don't help him none either.

587
00:36:29.137 --> 00:36:30.957
Get out of my way.

588
00:36:35.177 --> 00:36:37.917
You belong behind a plow.

589
00:36:58.557 --> 00:36:59.777
Who's Carson?

590
00:36:59.857 --> 00:37:01.077
Don't get down.

591
00:37:01.157 --> 00:37:03.597
We're here to take on those
saddle traps on this spread.

592
00:37:03.677 --> 00:37:04.677
Vamu!

593
00:37:07.157 --> 00:37:10.157
Are you a thief? I said get moving, tramp.

594
00:37:10.237 --> 00:37:11.417
Nice, Dan.

595
00:37:11.697 --> 00:37:15.037
Yeah, what's up to you?
Take out the top two planks.

596
00:37:15.137 --> 00:37:17.337
What did you say? The top two.

597
00:37:17.417 --> 00:37:19.497
Let's 16 inches of water over there.

598
00:37:19.577 --> 00:37:21.397
Who says so?

599
00:37:22.097 --> 00:37:25.437
And who might you be when you're at home?

600
00:37:25.777 --> 00:37:26.797
Quirt Evans.

601
00:37:29.797 --> 00:37:33.777
Well, anybody might ride up
here and say they're Quirt Evans.

602
00:37:33.877 --> 00:37:35.797
He ain't knowed around here by sight.

603
00:37:35.877 --> 00:37:37.537
Anybody can say they're him.

604
00:37:37.617 --> 00:37:38.777
Sure could.

605
00:37:38.997 --> 00:37:41.297
But there's a QE stamp on his saddle.

606
00:37:43.497 --> 00:37:47.321
Well, take the top place out of
that head gate, you stupid idiot.

607
00:38:01.297 --> 00:38:03.617
I don't need all that extra water.

608
00:38:05.057 --> 00:38:06.477
Step up on that pony.

609
00:38:06.557 --> 00:38:07.797
Well, now, look here, Mr.

610
00:38:07.877 --> 00:38:10.017
Evans. I've done what you told me to.

611
00:38:10.097 --> 00:38:11.077
You sure did.

612
00:38:11.157 --> 00:38:12.797
Step up on that pony.

613
00:38:12.877 --> 00:38:17.289
Well, I don't know what kind of a
fellow tries to get along with everybody.

614
00:38:21.797 --> 00:38:27.397
beside me wait a minute maybe we better
take out a couple more of them planks Chuck

615
00:38:27.477 --> 00:38:33.117
I got ten times as much water as I need
that'll do for now well if you ever need me

616
00:38:33.197 --> 00:38:36.837
more Mr Evans all you got to
do is say so I figured that

617
00:38:52.397 --> 00:38:53.537
Homer!

618
00:38:53.977 --> 00:38:56.257
Homer, look at the balloon!

619
00:38:56.877 --> 00:38:59.297
Homer, all the ditches are full of water!

620
00:38:59.797 --> 00:39:01.157
Homer, look!

621
00:39:01.257 --> 00:39:02.457
Homer, look!

622
00:39:09.057 --> 00:39:10.197
Homer!

623
00:39:23.017 --> 00:39:25.137
Now let's start off from scratch.

624
00:39:25.317 --> 00:39:27.277
You got more water than you need.

625
00:39:27.657 --> 00:39:29.057
Absolutely right, Quirt.

626
00:39:29.137 --> 00:39:30.697
I mean, Mr. Evans.

627
00:39:31.577 --> 00:39:33.477
Say, do you mind if I call you Quirt?

628
00:39:33.557 --> 00:39:34.577
Not a bit.

629
00:39:34.657 --> 00:39:38.137
So it's no skin off your moccasins if
you let a little water over that ditch?

630
00:39:38.217 --> 00:39:39.837
Absolutely no, Mr. Evans.

631
00:39:39.917 --> 00:39:41.417
I mean, Quirt.

632
00:39:41.657 --> 00:39:43.577
You gonna be neighborly or not?

633
00:39:43.657 --> 00:39:45.957
Well, I sure am, Quirt.

634
00:39:46.037 --> 00:39:47.917
Well, that's the way to talk.

635
00:40:00.717 --> 00:40:01.717
Thomas!

636
00:40:04.857 --> 00:40:06.537
Frederick Koss. Howdy, Mrs.

637
00:40:06.617 --> 00:40:07.117
Worth.

638
00:40:07.157 --> 00:40:09.337
We always knew you'd change your mind.

639
00:40:09.417 --> 00:40:10.657
Yes, ma'am.

640
00:40:10.817 --> 00:40:11.817
I did.

641
00:40:11.897 --> 00:40:12.937
Thank you, Frederick.

642
00:40:13.017 --> 00:40:15.899
All of us here in the
valley are grateful to you.

643
00:40:15.997 --> 00:40:19.857
Worth, it's like I was saying, I ain't
got any use for all that extra water.

644
00:40:19.937 --> 00:40:22.290
Why shouldn't I let my neighbors use it?

645
00:40:22.917 --> 00:40:26.857
I believe that neighbors
should be friendly.

646
00:40:29.117 --> 00:40:31.764
What have you got there
on your neck, a boil?

647
00:40:32.537 --> 00:40:35.717
It may as well as I wrestled
that dead-burned boil.

648
00:40:35.797 --> 00:40:38.317
Have you tried putting soap
and sugar holders on it?

649
00:40:38.397 --> 00:40:41.297
No, ma'am, I haven't. You get down
this minute and come in the house.

650
00:40:41.377 --> 00:40:42.737
I'll fix it for you.

651
00:40:43.097 --> 00:40:44.917
That's right. Are you going to hurt me?

652
00:40:44.997 --> 00:40:46.577
No, that's a shame.

653
00:40:46.657 --> 00:40:47.957
It looks awful.

654
00:40:58.077 --> 00:41:01.897
Well, looks like your prayer
straightened everything out.

655
00:41:01.977 --> 00:41:04.817
So you think your gun
changed Frederick Carson, huh?

656
00:41:04.897 --> 00:41:06.317
Who says I pointed a gun?

657
00:41:06.397 --> 00:41:07.337
I do.

658
00:41:07.417 --> 00:41:10.977
Well, I didn't. Then he gave
in more easily than I expected.

659
00:41:11.457 --> 00:41:13.137
They remember this quote.

660
00:41:13.237 --> 00:41:17.777
The Lord moves in a mysterious manner
at times using strange methods and odd

661
00:41:17.857 --> 00:41:18.697
instruments.

662
00:41:18.777 --> 00:41:19.557
Me?

663
00:41:19.637 --> 00:41:21.557
Well, that would be odd.

664
00:41:25.497 --> 00:41:29.397
I just get a gnawing hankering
after good pies and truck like that.

665
00:41:29.477 --> 00:41:30.857
Oh, Freddy Carson. Yes, sir?

666
00:41:30.937 --> 00:41:32.437
I want you to have some of these.

667
00:41:32.517 --> 00:41:34.497
They're awful good with
coffee in the morning.

668
00:41:34.577 --> 00:41:37.157
Makes me want to go home
and shoot my Chinese cook.

669
00:41:37.237 --> 00:41:39.377
You just come back for
more anytime you like.

670
00:41:39.457 --> 00:41:41.097
There are always plenty. Thank you.

671
00:41:41.177 --> 00:41:42.497
Goodbye. Goodbye, Frederick.

672
00:41:42.577 --> 00:41:43.577
Goodbye.

673
00:41:47.557 --> 00:41:49.897
Man ought to see more of his neighbors.

674
00:41:51.517 --> 00:41:52.517
Hey,

675
00:41:52.737 --> 00:41:53.977
wonderful woman, that Mrs.

676
00:41:54.057 --> 00:41:55.917
Worth. Look, she fixed my boil.

677
00:41:56.017 --> 00:41:57.017
Penny!

678
00:41:57.437 --> 00:41:59.517
Excuse me, I think Father
wants to give thanks.

679
00:41:59.597 --> 00:42:00.717
Oh, he already thanked me.

680
00:42:00.797 --> 00:42:03.856
I know. I think now he'd
like to thank someone else.

681
00:42:06.397 --> 00:42:09.044
Hold my plunder while
I get aboard, will you?

682
00:42:19.837 --> 00:42:23.417
You know, Mrs. Worth's gonna
keep me supplied in pies.

683
00:42:23.797 --> 00:42:27.091
I'm gonna bring her over a
quarter of beef now and then.

684
00:42:27.297 --> 00:42:29.997
Finally come to an
understanding with my neighbors.

685
00:42:30.077 --> 00:42:31.297
Sure did.

686
00:42:32.297 --> 00:42:35.957
Oh, well, of course. You know I was just
scared to death when I made that speech to

687
00:42:36.037 --> 00:42:37.037
them people.

688
00:42:37.477 --> 00:42:39.397
I'm glad you made me do it.

689
00:42:39.617 --> 00:42:41.337
Makes me feel good.

690
00:42:42.397 --> 00:42:43.637
Adios, amigo.

691
00:42:54.137 --> 00:42:55.137
Dinner.

692
00:43:05.417 --> 00:43:07.357
How bad's dinner going to be?

693
00:43:07.757 --> 00:43:09.857
Oh, roast, venison, and vegetables.

694
00:43:09.937 --> 00:43:12.117
And, of course, a skimpy chicken.

695
00:43:12.297 --> 00:43:15.121
Well, with a pie and cake,
I'll try to make out.

696
00:43:15.677 --> 00:43:17.537
Chicken or venison? Both.

697
00:43:17.617 --> 00:43:19.317
I feel real good.

698
00:43:33.917 --> 00:43:38.857
hmm new dress oh it's
first day you call it Sunday

699
00:43:39.297 --> 00:43:44.277
Kurt do me a favor and hitch up the team
for father sure and will you do me another

700
00:43:44.357 --> 00:43:50.777
favor go for a ride with us where
you're not to ask it's a surprise

701
00:43:50.857 --> 00:43:52.357
it's a pleasant

702
00:43:58.037 --> 00:44:00.797
Hey, Quirt, we got a surprise for you.

703
00:44:00.877 --> 00:44:02.517
He knows. I told him.

704
00:44:04.637 --> 00:44:06.717
Thought you weren't
allowed to work on Sunday.

705
00:44:06.797 --> 00:44:09.577
Oh, Quirt, there's nothing
we're not allowed to do.

706
00:44:09.657 --> 00:44:12.617
It's just that we don't believe
in doing what we know is wrong.

707
00:44:12.697 --> 00:44:15.077
Well, that makes it pretty
much each fellow's own guess.

708
00:44:15.157 --> 00:44:17.357
But each fellow knows inside.

709
00:44:17.437 --> 00:44:21.731
Well, there's a lot of gents I wouldn't
want to give that much leeway to.

710
00:44:46.437 --> 00:44:48.657
I didn't mean to frighten him, miss.

711
00:44:48.877 --> 00:44:50.337
Is there a...

712
00:44:50.477 --> 00:44:53.077
Is there a fellow named
Quirt Evans around here?

713
00:44:53.157 --> 00:44:54.177
Well,

714
00:44:54.577 --> 00:44:56.537
yes, I'll get him. Never mind.

715
00:44:56.737 --> 00:44:58.517
I can do more talking to you.

716
00:45:00.017 --> 00:45:01.617
Has he been here long?

717
00:45:01.837 --> 00:45:04.137
About three weeks, I guess.

718
00:45:04.297 --> 00:45:09.937
He didn't, uh... He didn't disappear
for, say, two days during that time,

719
00:45:10.017 --> 00:45:11.017
did he?

720
00:45:11.737 --> 00:45:12.497
No.

721
00:45:12.577 --> 00:45:14.497
Hello, you weather-beaten old hangman.

722
00:45:14.577 --> 00:45:15.437
Hi, man.

723
00:45:15.517 --> 00:45:18.477
Penny, this is Whistful McClinick,
the Marshal of the Territory.

724
00:45:18.557 --> 00:45:20.597
Hi, miss. And don't let
that gray hair fool you.

725
00:45:20.677 --> 00:45:22.117
He's a curly wolf.

726
00:45:22.237 --> 00:45:23.337
Hello, Quirt.

727
00:45:23.657 --> 00:45:26.077
See, I thought I had you about half hung.

728
00:45:26.557 --> 00:45:27.817
Tall fella,

729
00:45:28.077 --> 00:45:31.489
fast with a gun, held up the
Baker stage, killed a couple.

730
00:45:32.217 --> 00:45:36.097
Now, you're right tall, and
some say you're a fast man.

731
00:45:37.217 --> 00:45:40.746
But I guess I'll have to take
the little lady's word for it.

732
00:45:41.857 --> 00:45:45.957
When are you and Laredo Stevens going
to get around to killing one another?

733
00:45:46.037 --> 00:45:49.657
Laredo? Why, we water our
horses out of the same trough.

734
00:45:49.737 --> 00:45:52.972
Well, I'm sure looking forward
to hanging the survivor.

735
00:45:54.157 --> 00:45:56.377
You know, I'm a figuring man.

736
00:45:57.097 --> 00:46:00.617
Sometimes I don't know all the answers.

737
00:46:01.757 --> 00:46:04.397
Like that fella, Quirt Evans.

738
00:46:04.897 --> 00:46:10.497
Handyman with a coat. while he was acting
as Wyatt Earp's deputy over the tombstone,

739
00:46:10.657 --> 00:46:16.537
one day he turns in his badge and starts
building himself a nice cattle spread.

740
00:46:17.877 --> 00:46:21.877
Suddenly sells out and goes on to prod.

741
00:46:24.357 --> 00:46:25.557
Why?

742
00:46:26.377 --> 00:46:28.517
That's when I ain't figured yet.

743
00:46:28.597 --> 00:46:30.597
Sometimes things connect.

744
00:46:31.457 --> 00:46:35.697
About that time,
a fellow called Walt Ennis.

745
00:46:36.017 --> 00:46:38.193
went down in front of Laredo Stevens.

746
00:46:38.677 --> 00:46:43.677
Walt was recent at the time, and some say
that a gambler standing there grabbed his

747
00:46:43.757 --> 00:46:44.717
hand.

748
00:46:44.797 --> 00:46:46.617
Maybe that's connected.

749
00:46:48.577 --> 00:46:50.697
How did you stand with Walt Ennis?

750
00:46:51.117 --> 00:46:52.457
Know him.

751
00:46:53.397 --> 00:46:55.217
Two things connected?

752
00:46:55.917 --> 00:46:57.837
I know a lot of people.

753
00:46:57.917 --> 00:46:58.957
Well,

754
00:46:59.637 --> 00:47:01.097
I'll figure it out.

755
00:47:01.417 --> 00:47:02.837
I usually do.

756
00:47:03.657 --> 00:47:04.937
I'm patient.

757
00:47:06.557 --> 00:47:07.737
Young lady,

758
00:47:08.097 --> 00:47:12.273
don't be looking at him with your
eyes all bugged out like a cowbird's.

759
00:47:12.817 --> 00:47:14.637
There ain't no future in it.

760
00:47:15.297 --> 00:47:16.677
So long, folks.

761
00:47:19.977 --> 00:47:22.337
Quirt, please stay away
from Laredo Stevens.

762
00:47:22.417 --> 00:47:23.757
He owes me money.

763
00:47:23.837 --> 00:47:26.249
And don't worry, I might come out on top.

764
00:47:26.697 --> 00:47:28.237
That'd be even worse.

765
00:47:28.317 --> 00:47:31.788
Worse? And it'd be worse if
he goes down than if I go down?

766
00:47:32.337 --> 00:47:33.597
Don't you see that? Oh, I know.

767
00:47:33.677 --> 00:47:35.557
I'd be a guy with a marred soul.

768
00:47:35.637 --> 00:47:37.577
Don't make it sound so crude, Quirt.

769
00:47:37.657 --> 00:47:39.997
You see why? I couldn't love you.

770
00:47:42.317 --> 00:47:43.437
All right.

771
00:47:43.597 --> 00:47:45.597
I won't look up Loretta.

772
00:47:45.897 --> 00:47:47.377
It's better this way.

773
00:47:47.457 --> 00:47:52.717
Every time he opens the door, every time
he hears footsteps coming around a corner,

774
00:47:52.797 --> 00:47:55.077
Loretta will start sweating,
thinking that's me.

775
00:47:55.157 --> 00:47:57.804
His food won't sit well
the rest of his life.

776
00:48:01.877 --> 00:48:06.289
All right, but if I'm going to be holy,
I've got to get some fun out of it.

777
00:48:24.497 --> 00:48:27.197
You know, I feel like such a gadabuff.

778
00:48:27.277 --> 00:48:30.277
It seems hardly a whole
week since last Thursday.

779
00:48:48.217 --> 00:48:49.577
Hey, Quirt.

780
00:48:55.457 --> 00:48:57.217
I thought it was you.

781
00:48:57.837 --> 00:48:59.717
Well, well, well. Hello.

782
00:48:59.817 --> 00:49:02.357
